es":["English renderings of Russian female given names","English renderings of Ukrainian female given names","English terms borrowed from Russian","English terms borrowed from Ukrainian","English terms derived from Russian","English terms derived from Ukrainian"],"links":[["Russian","Катюша#Russian"],["Ukrainian","Катюша#Ukrainian"],["given name","given name"],["Катюша","Катюша#Russian"]],"glosses":["A transliteration of the Russian or Ukrainian female given name Катюша (Katjuša)"],"id":"a32f66b7-5fc6-4f3c-ab7c-02510c220ef0"},{"examples":[{"text":"The Russian Katyusha is a multiple rocket thrower of which nothing is known except what can be seen on released photographs.","ref":"1944, Willy Ley, Rockets: The Future of Travel Beyond the Stratosphere, Viking Press, page 77:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[12,20]],"id":"24e0b830-21b1-4e3e-95d8-7c4e6b2dfd1a"}],"links":[["artillery","artillery"],["rocket launcher","rocket launcher"],["rocket","rocket"],["Soviet Union","Soviet Union"]],"synonyms":[{"word":"multiple rocket launcher"},{"word":"Grad"},{"word":"Stalin's organ"}],"categories":["English terms with quotations"],"glosses":["A type of artillery rocket launcher consisting of an array of rockets mounted on a truck, originated in the Soviet Union."],"id":"03ab60b6-f255-4e94-aa1c-073542ffc1b8"},{"examples":[{"text":"The development of these projectors was spurred During World War II, the Soviet Union fired Katyusha rockets from the ground or truck mounted racks. The standard Katyushas were 6 feet long, weighed 92.5 pounds, and had a range of 3 miles.","ref":"1966, Wernher Von Braun, Frederick Ira Ordway, History of Rocketry & Space Travel, Crowell, page 88:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[92,100],[162,170],[162,171]],"id":"b3e5cf4e-7841-4872-9e70-c20574070ff8"},{"text":"A curfew was clamped down on December 21 upon the Arab village of Battir, south of Jerusalem, near where security forces on December 20 found the rocket launchers from which two Katyushas were earlier fired into a Jerusalem residential area.","ref":"1970, Muʼassasat al-Dirāsāt al-Filasṭīnīyah, The Arabs under Israeli occupation, Beirut: Institute for Palestine Studies, p 24","id":"75509921-5a85-43da-ba82-e4a1e0d47aec"}],"links":[["rocket","rocket"]],"synonyms":[{"word":"Grad"}],"categories":["English terms with quotations"],"glosses":["A type of rocket which can be launched from a Katyusha."],"id":"958c7463-5832-4c65-8e8d-8506a5b3b006"}],"related":[],"synonyms":[]},"katharevousa":{"id":"b8be8721-56a9-444d-8361-d1786a44b985","word":"Katharevousa","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"purist variant of Modern Greek","tags":["feminine","masculine"],"word":"catarévussa"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"purist variant of Modern Greek","tags":["masculine"],"word":"grego catarévussa"}],"pos":"name","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"‘You read the poetry of Cavafy, I have taught you to speak Katharevousa and Italian.’","ref":"1994, Louis de Bernières, Captain Corelli's Mandolin, Minerva, published 1995, page 87:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[59,71]],"id":"ce3f5cfc-4f07-4365-b410-d5052168aec0"},{"text":"A year later, after much deliberation and public discussion, an act of parliament established that demotic Greek was to replace the hybrid katharevousa as the official language of education – and therefore, in practice, in most walks of life (exceptions are the Church and law).","ref":"2019, Roderick Beaton, Greece: Biography of a Modern Nation, Penguin, published 2020, page 345:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[139,151]],"id":"e707fc0a-043e-4c23-a180-db64549e1948"}],"links":[["learned","learned"],["archaising","archaising"],["Modern Greek","Modern Greek"],["Classical Greek","Classical Greek"],["Demotic Greek","Demotic Greek"]],"coordinate_terms":[{"word":"Classical Greek"},{"word":"Demotic Greek"},{"word":"Ancient Greek"},{"word":"Koine"},{"word":"purist Greek"}],"categories":["English entries with incorrect language header","English lemmas","English proper nouns","English terms borrowed from Greek","English terms derived from Ancient Greek","English terms derived from Greek","English terms with quotations","English uncountable nouns","Entries with translation boxes","Pages with 1 entry","Pages with entries","Quotation templates to be cleaned","Terms with Afrikaans translations","Terms with Armenian translations","Terms with Esperanto translations","Terms with Finnish translations","Terms with French translations","Terms with Georgian translations","Terms with German translations","Terms with Greek translations","Terms with Hindi translations","Terms with Hungarian translations","Terms with Icelandic translations","Terms with Italian translations","Terms with Japanese translations","Terms with Korean translations","Terms with Mandarin translations","Terms with Mon translations","Terms with Polish translations","Terms with Portuguese translations","Terms with Russian translations","Terms with Serbo-Croatian translations","Terms with Spanish translations","Terms with Turkish translations","Terms with Ukrainian translations","en:Greece","en:Greek","en:Languages"],"glosses":["A learned, archaising form of Modern Greek, based on Classical Greek and used for formal and official purposes; it was legally displaced as the official language of Greece in 1976 by Demotic Greek."],"id":"a5c5e3ee-4388-4f8a-b750-18964df3497b"}],"related":[{"word":"Category of Katharevousa words"}],"synonyms":[]},"katydid":{"id":"fb2a41f6-e503-43e5-bd0d-3d0dfc43b696","word":"katydid","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"orthopteran of the family Tettigoniidae","tags":["masculine"],"word":"tetigoniídeo"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"orthopteran of the family Tettigoniidae","tags":["feminine"],"word":"esperança"}],"pos":"noun","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"I have noticed all these, and some at a much earlier date than the first faint lisping of a timid katydid; and all such sights and sounds are similarly suggestive—the summer is drawing to its close.","ref":"1889, Charles Conrad Abbott, Days Out of Doors, New York: D. Appleton and Company, page 194:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[98,105]],"id":"755e716a-d055-491b-b5d0-6ce63f2b8d24"},{"text":"In the night the katydids and crickets screeched in waves, soft then loud, back and forth.","ref":"2019, Colson Whitehead, The Nickel Boys, Fleet, page 52:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[17,25]],"id":"a5af9bd6-7c7f-49ba-8d41-9670412a0036"}],"categories":["American English","English countable nouns","English entries with incorrect language header","English lemmas","English nouns","English onomatopoeias","English terms with quotations","Entries missing English vernacular names of taxa","Entries using missing taxonomic name (species)","Entries with translation boxes","Pages with 1 entry","Pages with entries","Terms with Armenian translations","Terms with Cantonese translations","Terms with Catalan translations","Terms with Danish translations","Terms with Dutch translations","Terms with French translations","Terms with Galician translations","Terms with Georgian translations","Terms with German translations","Terms with Hungarian translations","Terms with Japanese translations","Terms with Korean translations","Terms with Mandarin translations","Terms with Navajo translations","Terms with Norwegian Bokmål translations","Terms with Norwegian Nynorsk translations","Terms with Portuguese translations","Terms with Russian translations","Terms with Spanish translations","Terms with Unami translations","en:Crickets and grasshoppers"],"links":[["cricket","cricket"],["Tettigoniidae","Tettigoniidae#Translingual"]],"synonyms":[{"word":"bush-cricket","tags":["UK"]},{"word":"tettigoniid","topics":["scientific","sciences"]}],"raw_glosses":["(chiefly US) A cricket of the family Tettigoniidae"],"glosses":["A cricket of the family Tettigoniidae"],"tags":["US"],"id":"982e8c1b-a3fa-49fb-bcfb-353689b06b5e"}],"related":[],"synonyms":[]},"katya":{"id":"ad1d810a-cf77-45cc-a805-52e099e921f9","word":"Katya","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"female given name","tags":["feminine"],"word":"Cátia"}],"pos":"name","tags":null,"senses":[{"categories":["English entries with incorrect language header","English female given names","English female given names from Russian","English female given names from Ukrainian","English given names","English lemmas","English proper nouns","English terms borrowed from Russian","English terms borrowed from Ukrainian","English terms derived from Russian","English terms derived from Ukrainian","English transliterations of Russian terms","English transliterations of Ukrainian terms","English uncountable nouns","Entries with translation boxes","Pages with 1 entry","Pages with entries","Terms with Belarusian translations","Terms with Danish translations","Terms with Faroese translations","Terms with Finnish translations","Terms with French translations","Terms with German translations","Terms with Hebrew translations","Terms with Persian translations","Terms with Portuguese translations","Terms with Russian translations","Terms with Swedish translations","Terms with Ukrainian translations"],"links":[["given name","given name"],["Kate","Kate#English"]],"glosses":["A female given name from Russian or Ukrainian, equivalent to English Kate."],"id":"019d5db3-d202-49c5-84fe-fd5a1b242e3d"}],"related":[],"synonyms":[]},"katorga":{"id":"d492fd8c-63da-4742-a410-c06fa88dbeac","word":"katorga","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"penal servitude in a Russian or Soviet labour camp","tags":["feminine"],"word":"katorga"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"a tsarist or Soviet labour camp","tags":["feminine"],"word":"katorga"}],"pos":"noun","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"Sentenced to ten years of hard labor in the Siberian mines, he defied the Russian tyrant by his funeral oration at the grave of Dmokhovsky, his boldness resulting in an additional fifteen years of katorga.","ref":"1912, Alexander Berkman, Prison Memoirs of an Anarchist:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[197,204]],"id":"957ca9f7-8ea0-41a8-b0dc-2729f6961f49"},{"text":"However, brutal floggings, increased terms of katorga, starvation diets, permanent chaining to a wheelbarrow and other fearsome sanctions failed to staunch the flow.","ref":"1991, Alan Wood, “Russia’s ‘Wild East’: exile, vagrancy and crime in nineteenth-century Siberia”, in Alan Wood, editor, The History of Siberia: From Russian Conquest to Revolution, London; New York, N.Y.: Routledge, →ISBN, page 124:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[46,53]],"id":"effcefe6-3460-4cf1-9e8c-5d62b21ec8e2"},{"text":"Under the Bolsheviks there was going to be an end to the katorga.","ref":"2007, Edward Crankshaw, Cracks in the Kremlin Wall:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[57,64]],"id":"e0c8fc7f-7761-49c5-8283-97f7c6c09ca3"}],"links":[["Penal","penal"],["servitude","servitude"],["Russian","Russian"],["Soviet","Soviet"],["labour camp","labour camp"]],"categories":["English terms with quotations","Quotation templates to be cleaned"],"glosses":["Penal servitude in a Russian or Soviet labour camp."],"tags":["countable","uncountable"],"id":"00e0d19a-893a-4e40-af77-33b59c2d7739"},{"links":[["tsarist","tsarist"],["Soviet","Soviet"],["labour camp","labour camp"]],"glosses":["A tsarist or Soviet labour camp."],"tags":["countable","uncountable"],"id":"8c85d4ab-af4c-43be-bbaf-f9d6f4e7f726"}],"related":[],"synonyms":[]}}
